    , zero
    )

{-| Exact decimal numbers: arithmetic that keeps every digit without
losing precision, and rounding only when you ask for it.

A `Float` cannot hold `0.1`. What it holds is
`0.1000000000000000055511151231257827`, which is close enough until you add
three of them and get `0.30000000000000004`. The addition is not at fault;
base two has no exact `0.1` to add. A `BigDecimal` works in base ten, so it
does:

    BigDecimal.fromString "0.1"
        |> Maybe.map (\d -> BigDecimal.add d d |> BigDecimal.add d)
        |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "0.3"

A value is a [`BigInt`](BigInt) and a power of ten: `unscaled * 10^-scale`.
There is no fixed precision anywhere, so `add`, `subBy` and `mul` never round.
They widen. The only operation that can fail to terminate is division, and
division and [`roundTo`](#roundTo) are the only operations that round a value.
[`toStringWithPlacesUsing`](#toStringWithPlacesUsing) takes a
[`Rounding`](#Rounding) too, but only to write a value down; it changes
nothing.


## One value, one representation

`1.50` and `1.5` are the same number, and in this module they are also the
same value. Every `BigDecimal` goes through a constructor that strips trailing
zeroes, so `==` is numeric equality.

    BigDecimal.fromString "1.50" == BigDecimal.fromString "1.5"
    --> True

This is the opposite of `java.math.BigDecimal`, where the two are equal by
`compareTo` but distinct by `equals`. We differ because Gren's `==` is
structural and cannot be overridden. A type whose `==` disagrees with its
`compare` is a trap in the language's most-used operator, and no amount of
documentation removes it.

The cost is that the scale no longer records significance. A `BigDecimal`
does not remember that a price was quoted to the cent. That is a formatting
question, and you ask it at the edge:

    BigDecimal.toStringWithPlaces 2 price --> "1.50"

@docs BigDecimal


## Making them

@docs zero, one, fromInt, toInt, fromBigInt, toBigInt, fromFloat, toFloat


## Reading and writing

@docs fromString, toString, toStringWithPlaces, toStringWithPlacesUsing


## Reading text you did not write

@docs fromStringWithin, Error


## Arithmetic

@docs add, sum, subBy, mul, negate, abs, powBy


## Dividing

There are two, because a decimal division either terminates or it does not,
and only the caller knows which outcome is acceptable.

@docs divBy, divByTo


## Splitting a total

Sometimes you need to divide a value into individual quantities that
will sum up to the original value, always. You cannot round, you must *share*
the remainder.

@docs allocate, allocateBy


## Rounding

@docs Rounding, roundTo


## Comparing

@docs compare, isZero, isNegative, isInteger, max, min


## The representation

@docs scale, unscaled, movePointBy


## A note on argument order

As in [`BigInt`](BigInt), wherever the order of the operands matters, the
subject comes **last**:

    a |> BigDecimal.subBy one -- a - 1

    a |> BigDecimal.divBy b -- a / b

`compare` is the exception, because it stands in for `Basics.compare`.

-}

import BigInt exposing (BigInt)
import Char
import Math
import Maybe exposing (Maybe(..))
import String


-- THE TYPE


{-| A number of the form `unscaled * 10^-scale`.

The unscaled value is a [`BigInt`](BigInt) and carries the sign, so there is no
size limit on the digits. The scale is an ordinary `Int` and may be negative:
`100` is stored as `1` with a scale of `-2`, because that is what stripping
trailing zeroes leaves.

Every value is built through a constructor that does that stripping, which is
what makes `==` numeric equality. Zero is `unscaled = 0, scale = 0` and nothing
else, so `x |> subBy x == BigDecimal.zero` is a reliable test.

-}
type BigDecimal
    = BigDecimal
        { unscaled : BigInt
        , scale : Int
        }


{-| Zero.

There is only one zero: a zero unscaled value is always given a scale of
zero, so `0.00` and `0` and `0e-40` are all this value.

It is also the divisor that makes every division in this module return
`Nothing`.

-}
zero : BigDecimal
zero =
    BigDecimal { unscaled = BigInt.zero, scale = 0 }


{-| One.

The step, and the numerator of a reciprocal: `one |> divByTo 20 HalfEven x`.

-}
one : BigDecimal
one =
    BigDecimal { unscaled = BigInt.one, scale = 0 }


{-| Build a value from an unscaled `BigInt` and a scale, normalizing it:
trailing zeroes are stripped from the unscaled value and the scale is reduced
to match, and a zero unscaled value is given a scale of zero.

Every value in this module is built here, which is what makes `==` numeric
equality.

-}
make : BigInt -> Int -> BigDecimal
make value places =
    if BigInt.isZero value then
        zero

    else
        stripChunks value places


{-| 10^7, the largest power of ten that fits in one of `BigInt`'s 24-bit
limbs, so dividing by it takes that module's single-limb path.

Stripping seven zeroes at a time before falling back to one at a time matters
for values that have many of them: `powBy 100 (fromInt 10)` arrives here with
a hundred to remove.

-}
chunkOfTens : BigInt
chunkOfTens =
    BigInt.fromInt 10000000


chunkOfTensDigits : Int
chunkOfTensDigits =
    7


ten : BigInt
ten =
    BigInt.fromInt 10


stripChunks : BigInt -> Int -> BigDecimal
stripChunks value places =
    when BigInt.quotRemBy chunkOfTens value is
        Just answer ->
            if BigInt.isZero answer.remainder then
                stripChunks answer.quotient (places - chunkOfTensDigits)

            else
                stripOnes value places

        Nothing ->
            -- Unreachable: the divisor is a literal ten million.
            stripOnes value places


stripOnes : BigInt -> Int -> BigDecimal
stripOnes value places =
    when BigInt.quotRemBy ten value is
        Just answer ->
            if BigInt.isZero answer.remainder then
                stripOnes answer.quotient (places - 1)

            else
                BigDecimal { unscaled = value, scale = places }

        Nothing ->
            BigDecimal { unscaled = value, scale = places }


{-| Ten to a non-negative power, as a `BigInt`. The one place this module
manufactures a scale change.
-}
powerOfTen : Int -> BigInt
powerOfTen exponent =
    if exponent <= 0 then
        BigInt.one

    else
        BigInt.powBy exponent ten


{-| The unscaled value a number would have at a scale it is being widened to.
The caller guarantees the target is no smaller than the current scale, so this
never drops a digit.
-}
at : Int -> { unscaled : BigInt, scale : Int } -> BigInt
at target value =
    BigInt.mul value.unscaled (powerOfTen (target - value.scale))


-- CONVERSIONS


{-| An ordinary `Int`, which is a whole number and so has a scale of zero or
less. Above 2^53 an `Int` is already inexact, so this converts the double you
passed, not the number you meant.
-}
fromInt : Int -> BigDecimal
fromInt n =
    fromBigInt (BigInt.fromInt n)


{-| Back to an `Int`, when it is a whole number and fits in one exactly.

There are two ways to get `Nothing`, and both are refusals rather than
roundings: there is a fractional part, or the whole part is past
`Math.maxSafeInteger`. Apply [`roundTo`](#roundTo) `0` first if you meant to
discard the fraction.

    BigDecimal.fromString "2.5" |> Maybe.andThen BigDecimal.toInt
    --> Nothing

-}
toInt : BigDecimal -> Maybe Int
toInt value =
    if isInteger value then
        BigInt.toInt (toBigInt value)

    else
        Nothing


{-| A [`BigInt`](BigInt), exactly and always.
-}
fromBigInt : BigInt -> BigDecimal
fromBigInt value =
    make value 0


{-| The whole part, truncated toward zero: `-3.7` becomes `-3`, the same
direction `BigInt.fromFloat` and [`Down`](#Rounding) go.

    BigDecimal.fromString "-3.7" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toBigInt
    --> Just (BigInt.fromInt -3)

For any other direction, apply [`roundTo`](#roundTo) `0` with the rounding you
want first.

-}
toBigInt : BigDecimal -> BigInt
toBigInt (BigDecimal value) =
    if value.scale <= 0 then
        BigInt.mul value.unscaled (powerOfTen (Basics.negate value.scale))

    else
        value.unscaled
            |> BigInt.quotBy (powerOfTen value.scale)
            |> Maybe.withDefault BigInt.zero


{-| The **exact** value of a `Float`, which is almost never the number that
was typed to produce it. `NaN` and the infinities are `Nothing`. Everything
else converts, because every finite double is an integer over a power of two,
and a power of two is a terminating decimal.

    BigDecimal.fromFloat 0.1 |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625"

Those digits are not noise. That is the whole of the double, and this is the
function that shows it to you, the same job `BigInt.fromFloat` does for
`1.0e30`.

That is also why this is the wrong function for a number a person wrote.
[`fromString`](#fromString) is the one for that:

    BigDecimal.fromString "0.1" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "0.1"

-}
fromFloat : Float -> Maybe BigDecimal
fromFloat f =
    if Basics.isNaN f || Basics.isInfinite f then
        Nothing

    else
        let
            -- Doubling is exact, and the significand is only 53 bits, so this
            -- reaches a whole number without ever leaving what a double holds
            -- exactly. It runs at most 1074 times, for the smallest subnormal.
            go value count =
                if value == Basics.toFloat (Math.truncate value) then
                    { value = value, count = count }

                else
                    go (value * 2) (count + 1)

            whole =
                go f 0
        in
        BigInt.fromFloat whole.value
            |> Maybe.map
                (\significand ->
                    -- f is significand / 2^count, and dividing by two is
                    -- multiplying by five and moving the point.
                    make
                        (BigInt.mul significand (BigInt.powBy whole.count (BigInt.fromInt 5)))
                        whole.count
                )


{-| To a `Float`, rounding to the nearest one. Rounding is all a `Float` can
do, and it is the reason this module exists.

Past a double's range the answer is an infinity rather than `Nothing`, because
that is what the arithmetic would have given anyway. [`toString`](#toString)
is the direction that never loses a digit, and [`toInt`](#toInt) is the
conversion that refuses.

-}
toFloat : BigDecimal -> Float
toFloat value =
    -- Through the decimal text, which gets the correctly-rounded double for
    -- free. Doing it arithmetically would round twice.
    String.toFloat (toString value)
        |> Maybe.withDefault 0


{-| Multiply by a power of ten by moving the point, not by multiplying: a
positive count moves it right.

    BigDecimal.fromString "1.5" |> Maybe.map (BigDecimal.movePointBy 2)
    --> BigDecimal.fromString "150"

This costs nothing at any size, because only the scale changes.

-}
movePointBy : Int -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
movePointBy count (BigDecimal value) =
    make value.unscaled (value.scale - count)


{-| The power of ten the value is stored over: `1.5` has a scale of `1`, and
`100` has a scale of `-2`.

This is the canonical scale, not one you chose. Because trailing zeroes are
stripped, it says how many decimal places the number actually needs, which is
a fact about the value rather than about its history.

-}
scale : BigDecimal -> Int
scale (BigDecimal value) =
    value.scale


{-| The digits, without the point: the `BigInt` that [`scale`](#scale) is the
exponent for. Together they are the whole of the representation.

    BigDecimal.fromString "-1.25" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.unscaled
    --> Just (BigInt.fromInt -125)

-}
unscaled : BigDecimal -> BigInt
unscaled (BigDecimal value) =
    value.unscaled


-- ARITHMETIC
--
-- None of these round. Addition widens to the finer of the two scales and
-- multiplication adds them, so the answer is exact and `Rounding` never comes
-- into it. Division is the one that has to be told what to do, and it is
-- under its own banner below.


{-| Add, exactly. The answer is carried at the finer of the two scales, which
is the one that loses nothing.

No `By` suffix, unlike [`subBy`](#subBy), because addition is commutative, so
there is no subject for a pipeline to put last.

-}
add : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
add (BigDecimal a) (BigDecimal b) =
    let
        places =
            Basics.max a.scale b.scale
    in
    make (BigInt.add (at places a) (at places b)) places


{-| Add up an array, exactly.

    BigDecimal.sum
        [ BigDecimal.fromInt 1990 |> BigDecimal.movePointBy -2
        , BigDecimal.fromInt 495 |> BigDecimal.movePointBy -2
        ]
        |> BigDecimal.toString
    --> "24.85"

A column of prices is the most common thing anyone does with these, and the
reason it is worth a function of its own is that nothing here rounds: the
answer does not depend on the order the values arrive in, which is exactly
what a `Float` cannot promise.

`sum` of an empty array is [`zero`](#zero).

-}
sum : Array BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
sum values =
    Array.foldl add zero values


{-| Subtract the first from the second: `subBy b a` is `a - b`.

    a |> BigDecimal.subBy one

-}
subBy : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
subBy b a =
    add a (negate b)


{-| Multiply, exactly. Commutative, so no `By`.

A product of decimals always terminates: the scales add, and the digits are a
`BigInt` multiplication. Exactness costs nothing here.

-}
mul : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
mul (BigDecimal a) (BigDecimal b) =
    make (BigInt.mul a.unscaled b.unscaled) (a.scale + b.scale)


{-| Flip the sign. The scale is untouched, so no arithmetic happens here at any
size.

`negate zero` is `zero`; there is no negative zero.

-}
negate : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
negate (BigDecimal value) =
    BigDecimal { unscaled = BigInt.negate value.unscaled, scale = value.scale }


{-| Drop the sign. Like [`negate`](#negate) this cannot fail and cannot
overflow, because a `BigDecimal` has no most negative value.
-}
abs : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
abs (BigDecimal value) =
    BigDecimal { unscaled = BigInt.abs value.unscaled, scale = value.scale }


{-| Raise to a power: `powBy exponent base`. A non-negative exponent is exact
however many digits it takes: the unscaled value goes through `BigInt.powBy`
and the scale is multiplied.

    BigDecimal.fromString "1.05" |> Maybe.map (BigDecimal.powBy 10)
    --> BigDecimal.fromString "1.62889462677744140625"

A negative exponent is a reciprocal, and a reciprocal usually does not
terminate. This gives the exact answer when there is one (`powBy -3
(fromInt 2)` is `0.125`) and `zero` when there is not. That is the same shape
of answer `BigInt.powBy` gives a negative exponent, and it comes with the same
warning: if `3 ^ -1` should be `0.333...` rather than nothing,
[`divByTo`](#divByTo) is the function that will say so.

-}
powBy : Int -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
powBy exponent (BigDecimal base) =
    if exponent < 0 then
        one
            |> divBy (powBy (Basics.negate exponent) (BigDecimal base))
            |> Maybe.withDefault zero

    else
        make (BigInt.powBy exponent base.unscaled) (base.scale * exponent)


-- DIVIDING


{-| Divide exactly, or not at all: `divBy divisor dividend`.

There are two reasons for `Nothing`, and the type does not distinguish them:
the divisor was zero, or the quotient does not terminate in base ten. The
second happens whenever the divisor keeps a prime factor other than two or
five after the common factors cancel, so [`isZero`](#isZero) on the divisor is
how you tell the two apart when it matters.

    -- 1 / 8 is 0.125, and stops
    BigDecimal.one |> BigDecimal.divBy (BigDecimal.fromInt 8)
    --> BigDecimal.fromString "0.125"

    -- 1 / 3 does not
    BigDecimal.one |> BigDecimal.divBy (BigDecimal.fromInt 3)
    --> Nothing

Use this when a wrong answer is worse than no answer (splitting a total,
converting a unit), and [`divByTo`](#divByTo) when you have a number of places
in mind and are willing to say so.

-}
divBy : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> Maybe BigDecimal
divBy (BigDecimal divisor) (BigDecimal dividend) =
    if BigInt.isZero divisor.unscaled then
        Nothing

    else
        let
            -- The quotient terminates in k places at most, where k counts
            -- whichever of two and five the divisor holds more of. Multiplying
            -- the dividend by 10^k and asking for an exact integer division
            -- both tests that and produces the digits.
            k =
                terminatingShift divisor.unscaled
        in
        BigInt.quotRemBy divisor.unscaled (BigInt.mul dividend.unscaled (powerOfTen k))
            |> Maybe.andThen
                (\answer ->
                    if BigInt.isZero answer.remainder then
                        Just (make answer.quotient (k + dividend.scale - divisor.scale))

                    else
                        Nothing
                )


{-| How many decimal places a division by this value can possibly need.

Write the divisor as `2^a * 5^b * m` with `m` coprime to ten. Then
`n * 10^k / d` is a whole number for `k = max a b` exactly when `m` divides
`n`, which is the same condition as the quotient terminating. That is why no
greatest common divisor is needed.

-}
terminatingShift : BigInt -> Int
terminatingShift divisor =
    Basics.max (factorsOfTwo (BigInt.abs divisor) 0) (factorsOfFive (BigInt.abs divisor) 0)


factorsOfTwo : BigInt -> Int -> Int
factorsOfTwo value count =
    if BigInt.isZero value || BigInt.isOdd value then
        count

    else
        factorsOfTwo (BigInt.shiftRightBy 1 value) (count + 1)


factorsOfFive : BigInt -> Int -> Int
factorsOfFive value count =
    when BigInt.quotRemBy (BigInt.fromInt 5) value is
        Just answer ->
            if BigInt.isZero answer.remainder && not (BigInt.isZero value) then
                factorsOfFive answer.quotient (count + 1)

            else
                count

        Nothing ->
            count


{-| Divide to a fixed number of decimal places, rounding the way you say:
`divByTo places rounding divisor dividend`.

This is the division that always has an answer, so the only `Nothing` is a
zero divisor.

    BigDecimal.one
        |> BigDecimal.divByTo 5 BigDecimal.HalfEven (BigDecimal.fromInt 3)
        |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "0.33333"

`places` is decimal places, not significant digits, and it may be negative:
`divByTo -3 HalfUp` gives an answer to the nearest thousand. Trailing zeroes
still come off afterwards, so a result that lands on `0.25` is `0.25` and not
`0.25000`; use [`toStringWithPlaces`](#toStringWithPlaces) if you need the
zeroes.

-}
divByTo : Int -> Rounding -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> Maybe BigDecimal
divByTo places rounding (BigDecimal divisor) (BigDecimal dividend) =
    if BigInt.isZero divisor.unscaled then
        Nothing

    else
        let
            -- The quotient scaled up by 10^places, as one integer over
            -- another. Whichever side the power of ten belongs on, it goes on
            -- before the division, so there is exactly one rounding.
            shift =
                divisor.scale - dividend.scale + places
        in
        if shift >= 0 then
            Just
                (make
                    (roundedQuotient rounding divisor.unscaled (BigInt.mul dividend.unscaled (powerOfTen shift)))
                    places
                )

        else
            Just
                (make
                    (roundedQuotient rounding
                        (BigInt.mul divisor.unscaled (powerOfTen (Basics.negate shift)))
                        dividend.unscaled
                    )
                    places
                )


-- SPLITTING A TOTAL
--
-- Division answers "what is each share", and answers it for one share at a
-- time. That is why the shares stop adding up: `10.00 / 3` is `3.33` to the
-- cent however it is rounded, and three of those is `9.99`. The missing cent
-- is not a rounding error to be thrown away. `allocate` and `allocateBy`
-- are for that.


{-| Split a total into parts that add back up to it:
`allocate places parts total`.

`places` says where the smallest unit is: `2` for a currency with cents, `0`
for one without, and it may be negative to hand out whole hundreds.

    BigDecimal.fromString "10.00"
        |> Maybe.andThen (BigDecimal.allocate 2 3)
        |> Maybe.map (Array.map BigDecimal.toString)
    --> Just [ "3.34", "3.33", "3.33" ]

Compare what dividing gives you. `divByTo 2 HalfUp` makes every share `3.33`,
and three times `3.33` is `9.99`; the cent is gone and no rounding mode brings
it back. Here the parts differ by one unit at the most, the earliest ones take
the extra, and the sum is the total you passed in.

There are two refusals, and both are refusals rather than roundings:

  - `parts` is less than one. There is nowhere to put the money.
  - `total` is not a whole number of units at `places`. `allocate 2` will not
    split `10.005`, because a third of a cent is not something to hand
    anybody. Round first and own the rounding:
    `allocate 2 3 (roundTo 2 HalfUp total)`.

A negative total splits away from zero the same way, so the parts of
`negate total` are the negated parts of `total`.

-}
allocate : Int -> Int -> BigDecimal -> Maybe (Array BigDecimal)
allocate places parts total =
    if parts < 1 then
        Nothing

    else
        unitsAt places total
            |> Maybe.andThen
                (\units ->
                    BigInt.quotRemBy (BigInt.fromInt parts) units
                        |> Maybe.map
                            (\answer ->
                                let
                                    -- The remainder carries the sign of the
                                    -- total, and its size is below `parts` by
                                    -- construction, so it is safe as an `Int`
                                    -- and it never asks for more units than
                                    -- there are parts to give them to.
                                    extras =
                                        BigInt.abs answer.remainder
                                            |> BigInt.toInt
                                            |> Maybe.withDefault 0

                                    bumped =
                                        BigInt.add answer.quotient (unitStep units)
                                in
                                Array.initialize parts
                                    0
                                    (\index ->
                                        if index < extras then
                                            make bumped places

                                        else
                                            make answer.quotient places
                                    )
                            )
                )


{-| Split a total in proportion to a set of weights, and still have the parts
add back up to it: `allocateBy places weights total`.

This is the one a cart wants. An order-level discount comes off the lines, and
it has to come off them exactly, or the lines no longer explain the total.

    -- 10.00 off, shared out over three lines by what they cost
    BigDecimal.fromString "10.00"
        |> Maybe.andThen (BigDecimal.allocateBy 2 [ line1, line2, line3 ])

Weights are relative, so `[ 1, 1, 2 ]` and `[ 25, 25, 50 ]` split alike and
the scales they are written at do not matter. Each part is its exact share
rounded toward zero, and the units left over go to the parts whose discarded
fractions were biggest, earliest first. That is the largest-remainder method,
and it is what keeps the parts within one unit of each other's fair share.

    BigDecimal.fromString "0.05"
        |> Maybe.andThen
            (BigDecimal.allocateBy 2
                [ BigDecimal.one, BigDecimal.one, BigDecimal.one ]
            )
        |> Maybe.map (Array.map BigDecimal.toString)
    --> Just [ "0.02", "0.02", "0.01" ]

Give every weight the same value and you get the split
[`allocate`](#allocate) makes. Like `allocate`, this refuses a total that is
not a whole number of units at `places`. It also refuses weights that do not
describe shares of anything: an empty array, a negative weight, or weights
that add up to zero.

-}
allocateBy : Int -> Array BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> Maybe (Array BigDecimal)
allocateBy places weights total =
    Maybe.map2 (largestRemainder places)
        (unitsAt places total)
        (shareWeights weights)


{-| The total as a whole number of units at `places`, or `Nothing` if it is
not one.

The whole test is whether the scale is finer than `places`, and nothing more
is needed because the normal form keeps no trailing zeroes. At `places` of
`-2`, `500` arrives with a scale of `-2` and splits into hundreds, while `550`
arrives with a scale of `-1` and is refused.

-}
unitsAt : Int -> BigDecimal -> Maybe BigInt
unitsAt places (BigDecimal value) =
    if value.scale <= places then
        Just (at places value)

    else
        Nothing


{-| One unit in the direction the total points, so that the parts of a
negative total get their extra unit away from zero too.
-}
unitStep : BigInt -> BigInt
unitStep units =
    if BigInt.isNegative units then
        BigInt.fromInt -1

    else
        BigInt.one


{-| The weights as whole numbers at one shared scale, which is all the
proportions need. `Nothing` if there are none, if one is negative, or if they
add up to nothing, because none of the three names a share of anything.
-}
shareWeights : Array BigDecimal -> Maybe (Array BigInt)
shareWeights weights =
    let
        parts =
            Array.map representation weights

        common =
            Array.foldl (\part finest -> Basics.max part.scale finest) 0 parts

        scaled =
            Array.map (at common) parts
    in
    if Array.isEmpty scaled || Array.any BigInt.isNegative scaled then
        Nothing

    else if BigInt.isZero (Array.foldl BigInt.add BigInt.zero scaled) then
        Nothing

    else
        Just scaled


{-| Hand out `units` in proportion to `shares`: everybody gets their exact
share rounded toward zero, and the units that are left over go one apiece to
the biggest discarded fractions.

The comparison is on the remainders of the same division, so no fraction is
ever built. Ties go to the earlier weight, which is what makes equal weights
agree with [`allocate`](#allocate), and it is written into the ordering rather
than left to the sort being stable.

-}
largestRemainder : Int -> BigInt -> Array BigInt -> Array BigDecimal
largestRemainder places units shares =
    let
        whole =
            Array.foldl BigInt.add BigInt.zero shares

        floors =
            Array.indexedMap
                (\index share ->
                    when BigInt.quotRemBy whole (BigInt.mul units share) is
                        Just answer ->
                            { index = index
                            , units = answer.quotient
                            , dropped = BigInt.abs answer.remainder
                            }

                        Nothing ->
                            -- Unreachable: `shareWeights` rejected a zero sum.
                            { index = index, units = BigInt.zero, dropped = BigInt.zero }
                )
                shares

        handedOut =
            Array.foldl (\part running -> BigInt.add running part.units) BigInt.zero floors

        extras =
            BigInt.subBy handedOut units
                |> BigInt.abs
                |> BigInt.toInt
                |> Maybe.withDefault 0

        step =
            unitStep units
    in
    floors
        |> Array.sortWith byDropped
        |> Array.indexedMap
            (\rank part ->
                if rank < extras then
                    { part | units = BigInt.add part.units step }

                else
                    part
            )
        |> Array.sortBy .index
        |> Array.map (\part -> make part.units places)


byDropped :
    { index : Int, units : BigInt, dropped : BigInt }
    -> { index : Int, units : BigInt, dropped : BigInt }
    -> Order
byDropped a b =
    when BigInt.compare b.dropped a.dropped is
        EQ ->
            Basics.compare a.index b.index

        order ->
            order


-- ROUNDING


{-| What to do with a digit that will not fit.

Seven of them, in three groups. The first two say which way to go regardless
of how close the value was:

  - `Up`: away from zero. `2.4` and `2.6` both become `3`.
  - `Down`: toward zero, which is truncation. `2.4` and `2.6` both become
    `2`, and `-2.6` becomes `-2`.

The next two depend on the sign of the number rather than on how close it was:

  - `Ceiling`: toward positive infinity. `-2.6` becomes `-2`.
  - `Floor`: toward negative infinity. `-2.4` becomes `-3`.

The last three all go to the nearer value and differ only on an exact tie:

  - `HalfUp`: ties away from zero. `2.5` becomes `3`. This is the rounding
    taught in school, and the one most people mean.
  - `HalfDown`: ties toward zero. `2.5` becomes `2`.
  - `HalfEven`: ties to the even neighbor. `2.5` becomes `2` and `3.5`
    becomes `4`. Ties fall both ways instead of always up, so a long column of
    rounded numbers does not drift. This is banker's rounding, and it is what
    IEEE 754 does by default.

-}
type Rounding
    = Up
    | Down
    | Ceiling
    | Floor
    | HalfUp
    | HalfDown
    | HalfEven


{-| Round to a number of decimal places: `roundTo places rounding value`.

    BigDecimal.fromString "2.675"
        |> Maybe.map (BigDecimal.roundTo 2 BigDecimal.HalfUp)
        |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "2.68"

That is the example a `Float` gets wrong, and not because of the rounding:
the nearest double to `2.675` is slightly *below* it, so rounding that gives
`2.67`. Here the value is exactly `2.675` and the tie is a real tie.

Places may be negative, which rounds to tens or hundreds. Rounding to more
places than a number has does nothing at all. There are no trailing zeroes to
add, because a value never carries any;
[`toStringWithPlaces`](#toStringWithPlaces) is where padding lives.

-}
roundTo : Int -> Rounding -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
roundTo places rounding (BigDecimal value) =
    if value.scale <= places then
        BigDecimal value

    else
        make
            (roundedQuotient rounding (powerOfTen (value.scale - places)) value.unscaled)
            places


{-| Divide two `BigInt`s to a whole number, deciding the last one by the given
rounding rather than by throwing the remainder away.

Everything here is phrased as "toward zero" or "away from zero" rather than in
terms of the sign, because the truncating division underneath already gives
the toward-zero answer; the only question is whether to take one more step out.

-}
roundedQuotient : Rounding -> BigInt -> BigInt -> BigInt
roundedQuotient rounding divisor dividend =
    when BigInt.quotRemBy divisor dividend is
        Nothing ->
            -- Unreachable: every caller has already rejected a zero divisor.
            BigInt.zero

        Just answer ->
            if BigInt.isZero answer.remainder then
                answer.quotient

            else
                let
                    negativeQuotient =
                        BigInt.isNegative dividend /= BigInt.isNegative divisor

                    away =
                        if negativeQuotient then
                            answer.quotient |> BigInt.subBy BigInt.one

                        else
                            BigInt.add answer.quotient BigInt.one

                    -- Twice the remainder against the divisor: the tie test,
                    -- done without a division of its own.
                    half =
                        BigInt.compare
                            (BigInt.mul (BigInt.fromInt 2) (BigInt.abs answer.remainder))
                            (BigInt.abs divisor)
                in
                when rounding is
                    Up ->
                        away

                    Down ->
                        answer.quotient

                    Ceiling ->
                        if negativeQuotient then
                            answer.quotient

                        else
                            away

                    Floor ->
                        if negativeQuotient then
                            away

                        else
                            answer.quotient

                    HalfUp ->
                        if half == LT then
                            answer.quotient

                        else
                            away

                    HalfDown ->
                        if half == GT then
                            away

                        else
                            answer.quotient

                    HalfEven ->
                        when half is
                            LT ->
                                answer.quotient

                            GT ->
                                away

                            EQ ->
                                if BigInt.isEven answer.quotient then
                                    answer.quotient

                                else
                                    away


-- COMPARING


{-| How the first compares to the second, the way `Basics.compare` does.

Because trailing zeroes are stripped, `compare a b == EQ` and `a == b` agree
about everything, which is the property this module is arranged around.

-}
compare : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> Order
compare (BigDecimal a) (BigDecimal b) =
    if signOf a /= signOf b then
        Basics.compare (signOf a) (signOf b)

    else
        let
            places =
                Basics.max a.scale b.scale
        in
        BigInt.compare (at places a) (at places b)


signOf : { unscaled : BigInt, scale : Int } -> Int
signOf value =
    if BigInt.isZero value.unscaled then
        0

    else if BigInt.isNegative value.unscaled then
        -1

    else
        1


{-| Whether this is zero, which costs nothing at any size: normalization leaves
exactly one zero, so this is a look at the unscaled value and nothing else.
-}
isZero : BigDecimal -> Bool
isZero (BigDecimal value) =
    BigInt.isZero value.unscaled


{-| Zero is not negative.
-}
isNegative : BigDecimal -> Bool
isNegative (BigDecimal value) =
    BigInt.isNegative value.unscaled


{-| Whether there is nothing after the point.

The test is simply whether the scale is zero or less, and that only works
because of normalization: a value that ends in a zero has already had it
removed, so `1.50` arrives here as `1.5` and `2.00` as `2`.

    BigDecimal.fromString "2.00" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.isInteger
    --> Just True

-}
isInteger : BigDecimal -> Bool
isInteger (BigDecimal value) =
    value.scale <= 0


{-| The larger of the two, standing in for `Basics.max`.
-}
max : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
max a b =
    if compare a b == LT then
        b

    else
        a


{-| The smaller of the two, standing in for `Basics.min`.
-}
min : BigDecimal -> BigDecimal -> BigDecimal
min a b =
    if compare a b == GT then
        b

    else
        a


-- READING


{-| Read a decimal number: an optional sign, digits with an optional point
somewhere among them, and an optional `e` exponent.

    BigDecimal.fromString "-0.001" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "-0.001"

    BigDecimal.fromString "1.5e3" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "1500"

    BigDecimal.fromString "+2.50" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "2.5"

The sign may be `+` as well as `-`, as it may in `BigInt.fromString`, and
there is at most one of it: `++2.5` and `+ 2.5` are both `Nothing`.

The `e` may be either case and the exponent may carry its own sign, so
`1.5E-3` is `0.0015` and `1e+5` is `100000`. The exponent is not bounded:
`1e-9999999999` is accepted, and would take ten billion digits to write. For
text you did not write, [`fromStringWithin`](#fromStringWithin) refuses that
before building it. [`toString`](#toString) never writes an exponent back
out; `1e-9` comes back as `0.000000001`.

An underscore between two digits is ignored, as in `BigInt.fromString`, so
`1_000.000_1` parses. One anywhere else, next to the point, the sign or the
`e`, makes the whole string `Nothing`. Unlike that function there are no
`0x`, `0b` or `0o` prefixes; a hexadecimal fraction is not something anyone
needs.

Anything else is `Nothing` rather than zero: no digits at all, a second
point, an `e` with nothing after it, a trailing unit:

    BigDecimal.fromString "1.2.3" --> Nothing

    BigDecimal.fromString "3 apples" --> Nothing

The point is always `.` and there is no grouping separator but `_`, as in a
Gren literal. A comma is not a digit, so `1,234.56` and `1.234,56` are both
`Nothing`. That is deliberate: `1,234` is a thousand in one country and a bit
over one in another, and no parser can tell from the text alone. Normalize a
localized string before it gets here, where the locale is known:

    german text =
        text
            |> String.replace "." ""
            |> String.replace "," "."
            |> BigDecimal.fromString

    -- "1.234,56" --> Just 1234.56

The same two replacements cover most of Europe. Grouping varies more than the
decimal separator does: French writes `1 234,56` with a space or a narrow
no-break space, Swiss German writes `1'234.56`, and Indian English groups
unevenly as `12,34,567.89`. Strip whatever the locale groups with, swap its
decimal separator for `.`, and this function does the rest.

This is the function for a number a person wrote down.
[`fromFloat`](#fromFloat) tells you what a double is really holding, which is
a different question.

-}
fromString : String -> Maybe BigDecimal
fromString text =
    scan text
        |> Maybe.map build


{-| Why [`fromStringWithin`](#fromStringWithin) refused.

`NotANumber` is everything the plain [`fromString`](#fromString) returns
`Nothing` for. `TooLong` is a well-formed number that would take more digits
to write out than the limit allows, and it says how many.

-}
type Error
    = NotANumber
    | TooLong { digits : Int, limit : Int }


{-| [`fromString`](#fromString) with a ceiling on how many digits the number
takes to write out, for text that comes from somewhere you do not control:
`fromStringWithin limit text`.

The exponent is the hazard. `1e-9999999999` is thirteen characters and a
perfectly small number, and it needs ten billion digits to hold. The plain
`fromString` will try. This one works out from the text alone how many
digits [`toString`](#toString) would have to write, and refuses before
building anything if that is more than the limit:

    BigDecimal.fromStringWithin 10 "0.00123"
        |> Result.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Ok "0.00123"

    BigDecimal.fromStringWithin 10 "1e-9999999999"
    --> Err (BigDecimal.TooLong { digits = 10000000000, limit = 10 })

    BigDecimal.fromStringWithin 10 "1.2.3"
    --> Err BigDecimal.NotANumber

The count is the digits of the written number on both sides of the point:
`0.00123` is six, `1e3` is four, `1.5` is two, and zero is one. Leading and
trailing zeroes in the text are not counted, because the value does not keep
them. A limit below one refuses everything. Malformed text is `NotANumber`
whatever its length.

-}
fromStringWithin : Int -> String -> Result Error BigDecimal
fromStringWithin limit text =
    let
        measure scanned =
            let
                count =
                    digitsToWrite scanned
            in
            if count > limit then
                Err (TooLong { digits = count, limit = limit })

            else
                Ok (build scanned)
    in
    scan text
        |> Result.fromMaybe NotANumber
        |> Result.andThen measure


{-| What a scan of the text leaves: the digits of the mantissa, validated and
with the point and any separators removed, the scale those digits sit at
once the exponent is applied, and the sign. No arithmetic has happened yet.
-}
type alias Scanned =
    { negative : Bool, digits : String, scale : Int }


scan : String -> Maybe Scanned
scan text =
    let
        cleaned =
            dropSeparators text
    in
    if String.contains "_" cleaned then
        Nothing

    else
        when String.split "e" (String.toLower cleaned) is
            [ mantissa ] ->
                scanMantissa mantissa 0

            [ mantissa, exponent ] ->
                String.toInt exponent
                    |> Maybe.andThen (scanMantissa mantissa)

            _ ->
                Nothing


{-| The part before the `e`, at the exponent the `e` gave. The exponent moves
the point, which is a subtraction from the scale and no arithmetic on the
digits.
-}
scanMantissa : String -> Int -> Maybe Scanned
scanMantissa text exponent =
    let
        body =
            if String.startsWith "-" text || String.startsWith "+" text then
                String.dropFirst 1 text

            else
                text

        negative =
            String.startsWith "-" text

        accept whole fraction =
            if String.isEmpty (whole ++ fraction) || not (String.all Char.isDigit (whole ++ fraction)) then
                Nothing

            else
                Just
                    { negative = negative
                    , digits = whole ++ fraction
                    , scale = String.count fraction - exponent
                    }
    in
    when String.split "." body is
        [ whole ] ->
            accept whole ""

        [ whole, fraction ] ->
            accept whole fraction

        _ ->
            Nothing


build : Scanned -> BigDecimal
build scanned =
    let
        magnitude =
            BigInt.fromStringWithBase 10 scanned.digits
                |> Maybe.withDefault BigInt.zero
    in
    make
        (if scanned.negative then
            BigInt.negate magnitude

         else
            magnitude
        )
        scanned.scale


{-| How many digits [`toString`](#toString) writes for a scanned number,
worked out from the text alone.

Strip the leading zeroes, which are not digits of the value, and the trailing
ones, which `make` would fold into the scale. What is left is `m` significant
digits at some scale `s`. With `s <= 0` the number is an integer with `-s`
zeroes appended, so `m - s` digits. With `s > 0` there are `s` digits after
the point and either `m - s` before it or a single `0`, so `max m (s + 1)`.
Zero is one digit.

-}
digitsToWrite : Scanned -> Int
digitsToWrite scanned =
    let
        significant =
            trimLeadingZeroes scanned.digits
    in
    if String.isEmpty significant then
        1

    else
        let
            trailing =
                String.count significant - String.count (trimLeadingZeroes (String.reverse significant))

            m =
                String.count significant - trailing

            s =
                scanned.scale - trailing
        in
        if s <= 0 then
            m - s

        else
            Basics.max m (s + 1)


trimLeadingZeroes : String -> String
trimLeadingZeroes text =
    if String.startsWith "0" text then
        trimLeadingZeroes (String.dropFirst 1 text)

    else
        text


{-| Remove the underscores that sit between two decimal digits, and only
those. Any other underscore is left in place for `fromString` to reject.

This has to run on the whole text, before it is split at the point and the
`e`, because `1._5` would otherwise become the digits `1` and `_5`, which
join up into `1_5` and read as fifteen.

-}
dropSeparators : String -> String
dropSeparators text =
    if not (String.contains "_" text) then
        text

    else
        dropSeparatorsFrom text


dropSeparatorsFrom : String -> String
dropSeparatorsFrom text =
    let
        step char state =
            if char == '_' then
                { state | pending = state.pending + 1 }

            else
                let
                    kept =
                        if state.pending > 0 && state.afterDigit && Char.isDigit char then
                            ""

                        else
                            String.repeat state.pending "_"
                in
                { pending = 0
                , afterDigit = Char.isDigit char
                , out = state.out ++ kept ++ String.fromChar char
                }

        final =
            String.foldl step { pending = 0, afterDigit = False, out = "" } text
    in
    final.out ++ String.repeat final.pending "_"


-- WRITING


{-| The number in full, with a leading `-` when negative and no exponent
however far the point has to travel.

    BigDecimal.fromString "1e-9" |> Maybe.map BigDecimal.toString
    --> Just "0.000000001"

There are no trailing zeroes, because the value has none to write:
`toString` of `1.50` is `"1.5"`. [`toStringWithPlaces`](#toStringWithPlaces) is
the one that pads.

This is the lossless direction. A `BigDecimal` survives a round trip through a
`String` and does not survive one through a `Float`, so if you are storing
these anywhere, store the string.

-}
toString : BigDecimal -> String
toString (BigDecimal value) =
    if BigInt.isZero value.unscaled then
        "0"

    else
        let
            sign =
                if BigInt.isNegative value.unscaled then
                    "-"

                else
                    ""

            digits =
                BigInt.toString (BigInt.abs value.unscaled)
        in
        if value.scale <= 0 then
            sign ++ digits ++ String.repeat (Basics.negate value.scale) "0"

        else
            let
                -- One digit more than the scale, so there is always something
                -- to the left of the point: 0.001 needs three zeroes added and
                -- a fourth to be the whole part.
                padded =
                    String.padLeft (value.scale + 1) '0' digits
            in
            sign
                ++ String.dropLast value.scale padded
                ++ "."
                ++ String.takeLast value.scale padded


{-| The number with exactly this many decimal places, padded with zeroes if it
has fewer and rounded half-even if it has more.

    BigDecimal.fromString "1.5"
        |> Maybe.map (BigDecimal.toStringWithPlaces 2)
    --> Just "1.50"

This is where significance lives. A `BigDecimal` does not remember that a
price was quoted to the cent (`1.50` and `1.5` are one value), so the number
of places is something the formatter is told, by the code that knows what the
number is for.

Half-even because it is the rounding that does not drift, which is the right
default for a column of numbers and the wrong one for a receipt.
[`toStringWithPlacesUsing`](#toStringWithPlacesUsing) is the same function
with the rounding named.

A count of zero gives a whole number and no point, and a negative one rounds to
tens or hundreds and writes the zeroes out.

-}
toStringWithPlaces : Int -> BigDecimal -> String
toStringWithPlaces places value =
    toStringWithPlacesUsing places HalfEven value


{-| [`toStringWithPlaces`](#toStringWithPlaces) with the rounding said out
loud: `toStringWithPlacesUsing places rounding value`.

    BigDecimal.fromString "2.345"
        |> Maybe.map (BigDecimal.toStringWithPlacesUsing 2 BigDecimal.HalfUp)
    --> Just "2.35"

    BigDecimal.fromString "2.345"
        |> Maybe.map (BigDecimal.toStringWithPlaces 2)
    --> Just "2.34"

Both of those are correct, and they answer different questions. Half-even is
the better default because ties fall both ways, so a long column of rounded
numbers does not drift upwards; that is why it is the one the plain function
uses, and why a report should keep it. But a price is not a column. Shops,
invoices and most tax authorities round a half away from zero, and a total
that disagrees with the arithmetic a customer did by hand is a support ticket
whatever IEEE 754 has to say about it.

So `HalfEven` for a report, `HalfUp` for a receipt, and this is where you say
which. Nothing is rounded but the text: use [`roundTo`](#roundTo) if the
value itself should change, and note that a value rounded to `places`
already will come out the same through either function.

-}
toStringWithPlacesUsing : Int -> Rounding -> BigDecimal -> String
toStringWithPlacesUsing places rounding value =
    let
        rounded =
            roundTo places rounding value
    in
    if places <= 0 then
        toString rounded

    else
        let
            -- Back up to exactly `places`, which normalization has just taken
            -- away again. This is the one place the module writes a zero it
            -- does not store.
            digits =
                BigInt.abs (at places (representation rounded))

            padded =
                String.padLeft (places + 1) '0' (BigInt.toString digits)

            sign =
                if isNegative rounded then
                    "-"

                else
                    ""
        in
        sign
            ++ String.dropLast places padded
            ++ "."
            ++ String.takeLast places padded


representation : BigDecimal -> { unscaled : BigInt, scale : Int }
representation (BigDecimal value) =
    value
